From e68c8154a26f2e5d63dae859588dd0aefff52aba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 22:55:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Rewrite gutter detection based on how ScanTailor actually
 solves this - longest contiguous run, not aggregate blank fraction

Josh correctly called out that the pattern so far (patch one page, break
another, patch that too) was reinventing the wheel badly instead of
converging. Researched how established scan-splitting tools solve this
before touching the code again.

ScanTailor detects actual vertical LINE features (edge detection + Hough
transform) and picks the most central one, rather than treating a gutter
as a proxy for 'mostly blank column' the way this file had been doing.
The core insight: a real book gutter is a genuinely CONTINUOUS physical
feature running almost the full page height (the spine doesn't stop
partway down) - a fundamentally different thing from 'blank most of the
time in aggregate', which is what every distinct failure mode in this
investigation actually traced back to (a stray mark and an internal
index-column gap can both look similarly blank in aggregate despite one
being a real, continuous gutter and the other not).

Replaced the core per-column scoring signal with longest contiguous
vertical blank run (was: aggregate blank fraction). Kept the existing
near-max-tolerance + run-selection logic on top of it rather than
replacing everything in one pass. Recalibrated GUTTER_MIN_BLANK_FRACTION
for the new signal's different scale, checked directly against the real
book's actual distribution rather than guessed.

Validated individually against every distinct gutter shape found in this
investigation - wide plateau, narrow sharp peak, narrow gradual ramp,
shadow-darkened, stray-mark-fragmented, and the internal-index-column
false lead - all six correct under one unified algorithm, no per-case
patches. Re-ran the synthetic false-positive safety test (still correct).
Ran the full process-isolated pipeline against the real 64-page book:
64/64 pages split correctly, broad visual sweep across the actual output
file with no regressions found.

Full narrative in RESUME_PROJECT_NOTE.md.
